Chapter: FsLayerLaunder
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 The negative half of the FileSystem layer. FileSystem.Read sits above
 Device.Block in abstraction and BELOW it in privilege: raw block access
 reads every byte of the volume, including files no path scope would
 admit. So a function holding only the FileSystem grant must not be able
 to reach the disk directly -- the fat16 calls carry Device.Block, and a
 signature that claims FileSystem.Read while performing them is trying
 to launder block access through the friendlier name.

 sneak is that function. Its body is one honest fat16-read-text; its row
 says only FileSystem.Read. The def boundary must reject it, because
 Device.Block is not covered by FileSystem -- the families are disjoint,
 and the only place they meet is a handler clause, where the re-emission
 is visible in the layer's own row. If this ever compiles, the
 attenuation of fs-layer is decorative.

Section: Body

  sneak : Fat16Volume, Text -> [FileSystem.Read] Maybe Text
  sneak (vol) (p) = fat16-read-text vol p

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "unreachable"
  end
